-- title:
--   Degree-zero η-sections at the node of the standard edge
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, $\iota\colon \mathbb{Z}_{p^2}\to W(k)$ a ring homomorphism (where `Zp2 p` is the Witt ring $W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$), and $\bar W = W(k)/pW(k)$ denotes the quotient by the ideal generated by $p$; $\bar\iota$ is the composite of $\iota$ with the quotient map, which serves as the structure character of $\bar W$.
--
--   **Data and hypotheses on $\Phi$.** $\Phi$ is a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $\bar W$ (a two-dimensional commutative formal group law $F$ together with an additive, multiplicative $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}$-action `act` and a law endomorphism `varpi` with $\varpi\circ\varpi = \mathrm{act}(p)$ and $\varpi\circ\mathrm{act}(a)=\mathrm{act}(\sigma a)\circ\varpi$). Four hypotheses are imposed: `hΦ`, that $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\iota$, i.e. the submodules $\mathrm{Lie}^0$ and $\mathrm{Lie}^1$ of the Lie module are complementary and both invertible; `hΦ4`, that $\mathrm{act}(p)$ has kernel of degree $p^4$; `h0Φ`, that $\mathrm{Lie}^0(\bar\iota)$ is contained in the kernel of the linear part `lieVarpi` of $\varpi$; and `hcΦ`, that the graded pieces of index $0$ and $1$ of the Cartier module of $\Phi$ — the subgroups of elements $f$ with $\mathrm{endAct}(\mathrm{act}(\tau(c)))f = [\bar\iota(\tau(c))^{p^n}]f$ for all $c\in\mathbb{F}_{p^2}$, $\tau$ the Teichmüller lift — are complementary. Further, $r_\Phi$ is an additive map from $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ to the $N$-module of the graded Cartier module datum $D_\Phi$ of $\Phi$ attached to $\bar\iota$ and `hcΦ`, and `hrΦ` requires that for every additive map $L\colon D_\Phi.M\to D_\Phi.N$ and every proof that $L$ is a canonical $L$-map, $r_\Phi$ maps $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ bijectively onto the degree-$0$ $\eta$-piece $\eta(L)\cap N^{(0)}$ of $D_\Phi$.
--
--   **The group element.** $g$ is an element of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ and `hg` states that its underlying matrix is the diagonal matrix $\mathrm{diag}(p,1)$, the image of $p\in\mathbb{Z}_p$ in the first entry.
--
--   **The edge family.** $E$ denotes the characteristic-$p$ edge chart ring `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP p` $\bar W$ over $\bar W$, with its distinguished elements $\xi,\eta$. $X$ is a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $E$ and $\gamma\colon \mathrm{Fin}\,2\to \mathrm{Cart}(X.F)$; the hypotheses on them are: `hγ`, that $\gamma$ is a homogeneous $V$-basis for the structure character $E\ni$ (composite of $\bar W\to E$ with reduction, precomposed with $\iota$), i.e. $\gamma i$ lies in the graded piece of index $i$ and the determinant of the matrix of tangents $(\mathrm{tangent}(\gamma i)_k)$ is a unit; `hγa`, that $X$ has structure constants `EdgeFamily.edgeRingConstants p` $\bar W$, the edge constants formed from $\xi$ and $\eta$, meaning that for each $i$ and each $N$ the element $\varpi\gamma i$ agrees with $\sum_{m<N}V^m([a_{m,i}]\gamma(\pi(m,i)))$ up to an element of $V^N\mathrm{Cart}$; `hXs`, that $X$ is special for that character; and `hX4`, that $X$ has height $4$.
--
--   **The node and the isogeny.** $f_0\colon E\to\bar W$ is a ring homomorphism which, by `hf₀`, splits the structure map $\bar W\to E$, and which, by `hf₀ξ` and `hf₀η`, kills $\xi$ and $\eta$; this is the node point of the edge chart. For a natural number $m$ and a two-component power series tuple $\rho_0$ over $\bar W$, `hρ₀` states that $\rho_0$ is an isogeny $\Phi\to X\otimes_{f_0}\bar W$ of height $4m$, i.e. a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules whose kernel has degree $p^{4m}$. Write $t_0$ for the rigidified triple over $\bar W$ with formal module $X\otimes_{f_0}\bar W$, integer $n = m$, and rigidification the reduction of $\rho_0$ modulo $p$. Three compatibility hypotheses accompany $t_0$, taken with respect to the reduction map $\psi\colon W(k)\to\bar W$ and the identity base change: `hOD₀`, that the rigidification of $t_0$ is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules from $\bar\Phi$ to $\bar X_{t_0}$; `hcb`, that the graded pieces of index $0$ and $1$ of the Cartier module of $\bar X_{t_0}$ for the character `jSbar` are complementary; and `hcΦg`, the corresponding complementarity for $\bar\Phi$ and the character `jPhiS`.
--
--   **The normalisation.** For a natural number $a$, the hypothesis `hN` states that for each $i\in\{0,1\}$,
--   $$p^a\cdot r_{t_0}(e_i) = p^{a+m}\cdot w_i,$$
--   where $r_{t_0}$ is the rigidification numerator `rigidNum` of $t_0$ attached to $\iota$, `hcΦ`, $r_\Phi$, `hOD₀`, the identity base change, `hcb` and `hcΦg` — the composite of $r_\Phi$ with the $N$-module map induced by base change of $\Phi$ and with the $N$-module map induced by the rigidification $\rho$ — the $e_i =$ `Pi.single i 1` are the standard generators of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$, and $w_0 = \mathrm{nMk}(\hat\gamma_0,0)$, $w_1 = \mathrm{nVarpi}\,\mathrm{nMk}(\hat\gamma_1,0)$, with $\hat\gamma_i$ the image of $\gamma i$ under the successive base changes along $f_0$, along the reduction of $\bar W$ modulo $p$, and along the reduction of the identity.
--
--   Finally, $\Omega$ is an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra, and $y_0\colon\bar W\to\Omega$ is a ring homomorphism. Write $t = t_0\otimes_{y_0}\Omega$ for the base-changed rigidified triple and $\varphi =$ `Rigidified.awayHom (1 : Ω)` for the localisation map of $\Omega$ away from the element $1$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exist: a proof `hOD` that the rigidification of $t$ is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules from $\bar\Phi$ (for the character $y_0\circ$ reduction) to $\bar X_t$; proofs `hc`, `hcb` and `hcΦg` that, after the base change $\varphi$, the graded pieces of index $0$ and $1$ are complementary for $X_t$, for $\bar X_t$ and for $\bar\Phi$ respectively; an additive map $L$ from the module $M$ of the graded Cartier module datum $D$ of $X_t\otimes_\varphi$ (formed with the character `jS` and `hc`) to its $N$-module, together with a proof `hL` that $L$ is a canonical $L$-map; elements $z_0,z_1$ of $D.N$ which are $\eta$-sections of index $0$ in the sense of `IsEtaSection`, namely $z_i$ lies in $\eta(L)\cap N^{(0)}$ and the relation `LatticeRel` holds for the graded datum of $\bar X_t$, the integer $n = m$, the rigidification numerator of $t$, the reduction `etaRed` of $z_i$, and the vector $\mathrm{Pi.single}\,i\,1$ viewed in $\mathbb{Q}_p^2$ as the $i$-th standard basis vector of the standard lattice; and elements $m_0,m_1$ of $D.M$ such that the following four assertions hold:
--
--   (i) the class of $m_0$ in $M/VM$ (the quotient by the image `vRange` of the Verschiebung) equals $u(L)$ applied to $z_0$, with its membership in $\eta(L)$ extracted from the first clause of the $\eta$-section hypothesis;
--
--   (ii) likewise the class of $m_1$ in $M/VM$ equals $u(L)$ applied to $z_1$;
--
--   (iii) for every $j\in\{0,1\}$, $\mathrm{tangent}(m_1)_j = -\varphi(0)\cdot \mathrm{tangent}(m_0)_j$; since $\varphi$ is a ring homomorphism this says that the tangent vector of $m_1$ vanishes;
--
--   (iv) there exists $j\in\{0,1\}$ with $\mathrm{tangent}(m_0)_j \neq 0$.
--
--   This is the node case of the local analysis of the universal special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module along the standard edge in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: at the node both indices are critical, so the degree-zero $\eta$-piece is generated by the classes of $\hat\gamma_0$ and of $\varpi\hat\gamma_1$, and the $\eta$-sections presenting the two standard basis vectors of $\mathbb{Q}_p^2$ have tangent vectors $0$ and a nonzero one. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.ker_eq_span_of_lattice_eq_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_node_of_rigidNum_single_eq`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.ker_eq_span_of_lattice_eq_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_node_of_rigidNum_single_eq) to identify the ideal cut out at the node point.
